<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">J Cell Biol</journal-id><journal-title>The Journal of Cell Biology</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0021-9525</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1540-8140</issn><publisher><publisher-name>The Rockefeller University Press</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">14086758</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2106878</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>ISOLATION OF AN INSULIN SECRETION GRANULE FRACTION</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Lindall</surname><given-names>Arnold W.</given-names><suffix>Jr.</suffix></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Bauer</surname><given-names>G. Eric</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Dixit</surname><given-names>Padmaker K.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Lazarow</surname><given-names>Arnold</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="N0x19fb5d0N0x3776850">From the Department of Anatomy, University of Minnesota, Minneapolis, and the Marine Biological Laboratory, Wood's Hole, Massachusetts</aff><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><day>1</day><month>11</month><year>1963</year></pub-date><volume>19</volume><issue>2</issue><fpage>317</fpage><lpage>324</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>29</day><month>3</month><year>1963</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 1963 by The Rockefeller Institute Press</copyright-statement></permissions><abstract><p> Goosefish islets were homogenized in 0.25 <sc>M</sc> sucrose and separated into nuclear, mitochondrial + secretion granule, microsomal, and supernatant fractions. Eighty per cent of the cytochrome oxidase activity and 75 per cent of the bioassayed insulin activity were found in the mitochondrial + secretion granule fraction (6000 <italic>g</italic> for 10 minutes). The mitochondrial + secretion granule fraction was further subfractionated by centrifugation (2 hours at 100,000 <italic>g</italic> and 0&#x000b0;C) using a continuous linear density gradient 1.0&#x02013;2.0 <sc>M</sc> sucrose). Eighteen to 20 subfractions were collected by piercing the bottom of the tube and collecting drops. The total protein was distributed into a bimodal curve consisting of a high density component, which contained 90 per cent of the insulin (secretion granules), and a lower density component, which contained the cytochrome oxidase activity (mitochondria). </p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>